---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoRemux from '@/components/tools/media/VideoRemux.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="avi-to-mp4">
  <VideoRemux
    client:load
    from="avi"
    target="mp4"
    hint="Old camcorder captures, DivX rips and Windows-era clips, converted on your own machine"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Bringing a 2003 file into 2026</h2>
    <p>
      AVI is a Microsoft container from 1992. It is older than most of what modern video expects: it has
      no reliable way to store variable frame rates, no standard place for aspect-ratio metadata, and no
      support for the B-frames that modern codecs use. Many home videos, camcorder captures and
      DivX-era downloads are still AVI, and people need to open them on phones that do not support it.
    </p>
    <p>
      AVI to MP4 is almost always a full re-encode, not a re-wrap. The video is usually MPEG-4 Part 2
      (DivX, Xvid) or an older MJPEG or Cinepak stream, and modern devices decode H.264 and little else,
      so the frames must be rebuilt. This page inspects the file first and tells you which path it takes.
    </p>

    <h2>Common AVI problems and what fixes them</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symptom</th>
            <th>Cause</th>
            <th>Result after converting</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Phone or TV refuses the file</td>
            <td>The AVI container itself</td>
            <td>Fixed: all devices support MP4</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Video plays, no sound</td>
            <td>AC-3, ADPCM or a proprietary ACM audio codec</td>
            <td>Fixed: audio is re-encoded to AAC</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Green or blocky picture in a browser</td>
            <td>Xvid packed bitstream</td>
            <td>Fixed by the re-encode</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>People look stretched or squashed</td>
            <td>Non-square pixels with no aspect metadata</td>
            <td>Usually fixed; check the preview before saving</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Combing lines on fast motion</td>
            <td>Interlaced footage from a camcorder or TV capture</td>
            <td>Not fixed: this tool does not deinterlace</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Audio drifts out of sync over time</td>
            <td>VBR MP3 audio in a container that assumed CBR</td>
            <td>Usually fixed, because the audio is rebuilt with correct timestamps</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What to expect from the quality</h2>
    <p>
      Re-encoding always loses some quality. The AVI was already compressed once, and a second
      compression of the decoded result removes more. You control how much. The
      <strong>high quality</strong> preset (CRF 18) keeps almost all detail of an old standard-definition
      source and gives a file a little larger than the original. <strong>Balanced</strong> (CRF 23) is
      the default. <strong>Small file</strong> (CRF 28) is for clips you email, where the content
      matters more than the grain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Old footage is low resolution, usually 640×480 or 720×576, so encoding runs at about real time or
      faster on a modern laptop. The MP4 is usually much smaller than the AVI, because H.264 is a much
      better codec than those of 2003.
    </p>

    <h2>Archiving a shelf of old files</h2>
    <p>
      For a folder of family videos, convert at the high-quality setting and keep the originals.
      Storage is cheap, and a re-encode cannot be undone. Convert once, to a format you can still open
      in twenty years. H.264 in MP4 is currently the safest choice, and all phones, browsers, TVs and
      editors support it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conversion runs on your machine, so your family footage is not uploaded, kept on a server or
      linked to an account.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
